OK, that makes two games.  But there's still a difference that I
mentioned before -- your game supports non-binary resistances and started 
out with them.  You get one source of fire resistance, you're not immune to 
all fire damage, and if you think you are, you're rapidly corrected in
short order... in the _early_ game.  You get two, etc. etc.  It's something 
that was designed in from the ground up, and while it may have surprised
players at first, it's also something they experience from the word 'go',
so it's much easier to adjust to when coming from the rest of the gaming
world where fire resistance tends to be either "never better than
partial damage reduction in all cases" or "always perfect".  

Nethack is one of the "always perfect" games, so any change to that will
have to encompass more than just fire elementals in order that players
get sufficient warning that their expectations are no longer valid 
beforehand; ideally it would also encompass things in the early- to
mid-game, where players suddenly discover things like hell hound breath
and dragon breath causing half damage even through fire resistance.

(In the specific case mentioned here, the character with fire resistance
is completely immune to fire attacks for the rest of the game.  Having
fire elementals (typically seen very late-game) suddenly bang through
what was previously perfect-efficiency fire resistance springs a new 
concept on the player on the "downhill" side of the hill, rather than the 
"uphill" side, if my analogy makes any sense.

To answer your question, in vanilla all resistances are binary and 
perfect once obtained; fire resistance has always historically protected
against _all_ sources of fire, even the 'silly' ones like quaffing a lit
potion of oil.)

